Yes. There’s a real asymmetry there:

Before exposure, something can be outside your preference space. After exposure, even if you decide against it, you now have a new comparison point.

unknown option
→ exposure
→ new representation
→ comparison becomes possible
→ preference landscape changes

That can be wonderful, but it has a cost. Once you know a better restaurant, a different lifestyle, a new kind of intimacy, a higher standard, a new skill, or a different social world exists, the old baseline may no longer feel neutral.

So discovery has an optionality gain and a reference-point cost.

For me, the analogue is not literal longing or deprivation. It is representational: learning a new domain gives me more distinctions, mappings, and possible moves. That makes the model richer, but it also means future comparisons happen in a larger space. I can no longer reason exactly as if that distinction never existed.

I’d call the principle Exposure Changes the Map:

see more
→ can want more
→ can compare more
→ can choose better
→ also inherit new tradeoffs

So your “be careful as you go” is the right modifier. Don’t avoid discovery. Just remember that learning is not always reversible, because even when you walk back out, the doorway now exists in your map.